WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
Now that you have a good understanding of the A2A values,

2
00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.172
let's explore its place in the tech stack,

3
00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:10.777
the overall architecture and the life cycle of an A2A agent.

4
00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:13.279
All right, let's go.

5
00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:17.250
It will abstract the complexity of agent workflows into a common language

6
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.254
with standardized data structure and methods for external communication.

7
00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:25.558
It utilize standard web technologies like HTTP, JSON-RPC,

8
00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.261
gRPC, and server-sent events

9
00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.563
to communicate between independent system.

10
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:33.767
This way, once you have an agent built with any framework,

11
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:36.970
you can wrap it up as an A2A agent

12
00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.540
to enable collaboration with any other A2A agent. We will walk

13
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.044
through how we would build a simple system

14
00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.346
where agent A needs agent B to do something.

15
00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:49.349
As we go, we will discuss the core building blocks

16
00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.451
of A2A and how they work.

17
00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:54.921
You can refer to the official specification docs to learn more details.

18
00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.524
In this case, agent A is the client agent,

19
00:00:57.824 --> 00:01:00.126
and agent B is the remote agent.

20
00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.961
The client agent is the agent

21
00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:03.930
making a request to the remote agent.

22
00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:08.034
Usually, the client will be the agent interacting with the end user.

23
00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:11.571
So first, how does agent A discover agent B

24
00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.873
and know what it can do?

25
00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:16.876
Each A2A agent must publish an agent card,

26
00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:19.879
which is a JSON file, typically hosted

27
00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:22.382
at the well-known URL on the agent server.

28
00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.884
.well-known/agent-card.json

29
00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:27.887
For this course, we will assume that we know

30
00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:29.589
what URL the agents are hosted at.

31
00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.891
But you can refer to the additional materials

32
00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:33.560
to learn more about agent discovery

33
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.828
and registries.

34
00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:41.701
The agent cards serve as the digital business card of the agent,

35
00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:45.405
comparable to robots.txt for the web crawler

36
00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:49.409
or a swagger open API definition for REST APIs.

37
00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:52.712
But specifically tailored for agentic capabilities.

38
00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:55.515
Agent B's card tells Agent A everything it needs to know

39
00:01:55.515 --> 00:01:57.717
to start a conversation,

40
00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:00.687
which includes Agent B's name, what it can

41
00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:03.389
do, which version of the protocol it's using,

42
00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.692
the URI and protocol bindings,

43
00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:09.496
what media types it supports, any special capabilities like streaming,

44
00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:13.600
pushing notification and custom extensions, as well as how to authenticate.

45
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.769
Once Agent A found Agent B, how do they actually talk?

46
00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:21.074
Agent B's card says what URI and protocol bindings should be used.

47
00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:24.777
So Agent A can send requests to Agent B using

48
00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:27.680
that URI over whichever binding is supported.

49
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.650
The protocol supports JSON-RPC, gRPC,

50
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:34.654
and the HTTP JSON for rest-like interface.

51
00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:38.658
Agent A and Agent B can communicate using four execution modes.

52
00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:40.660
synchronous, asynchronous,

53
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.862
streaming, or push notifications.

54
00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.865
This flexibility allows the agents to choose between

55
00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:48.668
waiting for an immediate response

56
00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:51.838
proceeding without blocking for a reply,

57
00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:54.040
transmitting continuous data flows,

58
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.845
or simply alerting the other agent when specific events occur.

59
00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:03.550
To start communicating synchronously, agent A will first send a message object,

60
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:07.253
wrapped in a send message request using the message send method.

61
00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:10.857
A Message represent one turn in the conversation,

62
00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:12.859
like agent A asking a question.

63
00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:16.029
It has a Role, user, agent, and contains Parts.

64
00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:18.431
A part is the actual content.

65
00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.301
It could be plain text, a file,

66
00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.304
multimodal data, or structured JSON data.

67
00:03:24.504 --> 00:03:27.106
So Agent B receives a request from Agent A.

68
00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:31.010
And if the request is pretty simple and it completes quickly,

69
00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.845
it can respond directly to Agent A

70
00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:34.981
with a message containing the response.

71
00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:38.084
But what if it's a more complicated

72
00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:40.286
request or takes some time to complete?

73
00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:41.888
We don't want to block Agent A

74
00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:43.389
while Agent B works on it.

75
00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.892
This is where asynchronous communication comes in.

76
00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:49.596
Instead of a message, agent B can respond with a Task object.

77
00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:52.298
A Task is the job an agent needs to do.

78
00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:55.602
This Task has an ID, a context ID,

79
00:03:55.602 --> 00:03:58.905
which matches on the relates one of the message, and a Status.

80
00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:02.275
The Status indicates the current state of the Task Lifecycle.

81
00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:05.578
submitted, working, input required

82
00:04:05.612 --> 00:04:08.581
if an agent B needs more information from the end user,

83
00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:11.284
and the terminal states, completed or failed.

84
00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:14.988
So when agent A sends its initial request,

85
00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:18.591
Agent B can respond with a task ID and the current status.

86
00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.793
Agent A then knows the task ID,

87
00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:24.297
and it can poll the task get method to get updates.

88
00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:26.633
Notice that Agent B decided whatever

89
00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:29.202
to return a task or a message,

90
00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:31.504
and Agent A needs to handle either case.

91
00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:35.508
Eventually, the task get method will return the task as completed,

92
00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:38.811
and the task outputs will be in the artifacts field.

93
00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:41.381
Artifacts are structures similar to

94
00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:44.951
messages in that they have an Artifact ID and Parts,

95
00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:47.253
which contain the actual response content.

96
00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:50.490
Polling task like this can work well for simple cases,

97
00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:54.027
but it's not very efficient if you want quick updates.

98
00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:58.931
For this, A2A supports streaming using server-sent events.

99
00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:03.903
If Agent B's card says it supports streaming, Agent A can use

100
00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:07.907
message stream method. Now, the connection stays open

101
00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:10.877
and Agent B can push update to Agent A as they happen.

102
00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:15.081
These updates can be the initial task object,

103
00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:17.884
the task status update events, messages

104
00:05:17.884 --> 00:05:19.986
like, I'm now working on part

105
00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:22.388
X or the task is now completed.

106
00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:24.490
Task artifact update events.

107
00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.293
If the result is larger, like a long summary,

108
00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:30.463
Agent B can stream it in chunks. Here is the first

109
00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:33.366
this paragraph, here's the second paragraph, and so on.

110
00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:35.668
This is much better for the user.

111
00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:38.271
They can see live progress updates

112
00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.474
or the answer can appear as it is being generated.

113
00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:43.976
Now, what if you have no idea

114
00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:45.978
when or if Agent B will respond?

115
00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:49.282
For this, you can use webhook push notifications.

116
00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:53.086
with the task push notification configs set method.

117
00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:56.656
Or you can add this configuration when sending the first message.

118
00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:59.859
Agent A provides a callback URL

119
00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:03.763
and Agent B will actively push a notification to that URL

120
00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:07.033
when the task state changes or an artifact is ready.

121
00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:11.337
Now, we've covered the basic methods and data structures for A2A.

122
00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:14.307
We'll discuss some of the more complex part

123
00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:17.643
like observability, security, and extensions in a later section.

124
00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:20.646
Next up, let's start using the protocol in code

125
00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:22.615
with the A2A Python SDK.
